-- coroner_event.txt --
An event is something that happened at a moment or over an interval, as a source reported
it. Events go to, and are listed from, the timeline -t names, else CORONER_TIMELINE, else
the one 'coroner timeline use' picked. view, edit, delete, link and unlink also take an
event's id, from any timeline. list takes -t more than once to overlay several timelines.

USAGE
  coroner event [flags]
  coroner event <command> [flags]

COMMANDS
  add          Add an event to the current timeline
  delete       Delete events and their annotations
  edit         Change an event's text, time, source, tags or other fields
  link         Link one event to another, in any timeline
  list         List a timeline's events, filtered
  unlink       Remove the link of a kind from one event to another
  view         Show an event, its annotations and its links

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event add "Primary stepped down" --at 2026-09-11T03:14:07Z --source mongod
  $ coroner event list --tag election

FLAGS
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_add.txt --
Add an event to the current timeline, and print the time it resolved to.

A time with no offset is read in --tz, then the config file's default_timezone, then the
local zone. RFC 3339 is stable and natural language experimental, so scripts send RFC 3339.
Run 'coroner help time' for the times you can type.

Pass - to read the text from stdin, which is never read otherwise.

Each --attach copies a file, such as a screenshot, into the blob directory beside the
database, and prints its id, sha256, size and type. Every file is read before anything is
added, so one that cannot be read adds nothing.

--id with --at, and --author when USER may differ, gives the event an id and time of your
own, so running the same command again adds nothing and exits 0, while the same id with
different content exits 4.

--from-file adds a JSONL file of event records, as 'coroner timeline export --format jsonl'
writes them, in one transaction, to the timeline -t, CORONER_TIMELINE or 'coroner timeline
use' selects, which every record's timeline_id must name. Every record is checked first, so
one bad record adds nothing. A record carries its own id and RFC 3339 times, so running the
same file again adds nothing.

On a terminal, with no text or with --interactive, a form asks for the text, time, source,
tags and files, and keeps the other flags under More options, which opens by itself when one
of them holds a value. It suggests sources and tags, the ones on the timeline's latest
events first, previews the time as you type it, and prints the command each entry equals.
Under --json only --interactive opens it, and --no-input never does.

USAGE
  coroner event add {<text> | - | --from-file {<file> | -}} [flags]

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event add "Primary stepped down: replSetStepDown" --at 2026-09-11T03:14:07.412Z \
      --source mongod --tag election
  $ coroner event add "Dashboard at peak" --at 14:02 --attach ./p99.png --attach ./cpu.png
  $ pbpaste | coroner event add - --source slack --at "yesterday 23:02"
  $ coroner event add "Deploy started" --at "around 2026-09-11 14:00" \
      --ended "2026-09-11 14:20" --dry-run --json
  $ coroner event add "Election timeout on db-3" --id 01a0a07c-cc3e-764d-b6e9-6d030ebfc0b2 \
      --at 2026-09-11T03:14:30Z --json
  $ coroner event add -t mongo-outage --from-file events.jsonl --dry-run --json
  $ coroner event add --interactive --source mongod

FLAGS
      --approximate      Mark the time as a guess, as a ~ or around prefix does
      --at time          When it happened, as a time (default: now)
      --attach file      Copy this file in as an attachment; repeat for more
      --author name      Who is adding it, as a name (default: config author, then USER)
      --dry-run          Show the event as it would be added, and add nothing
      --ended time       When it ended, as a time, for an event that lasted
      --from-file file   Add every event record in this JSONL file, or - to read them from stdin, in one transaction
      --id uuid          The event's id, as a lowercase UUIDv7 uuid
      --interactive      Open a form to fill in, prefilled from the other flags (needs a terminal)
      --source source    System, service or person it came from, as a source, created on first use
      --tag tag          Keyword such as phase:detect, as a tag; repeat for more
      --url url          Permalink to the log line or message, as a url

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path        Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path            Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug              Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format      Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help               Show help for a command
      --json               Short for --format json
      --no-color           Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input           Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet              Print only essential output
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --tz zone            Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_delete.txt --
Delete events with their tags, annotations and attachments, and the blobs no other
attachment uses, then print each as it was, so its fields can be added again. Every event
is found before any is deleted: when one does not exist, nothing is deleted and delete
exits 3. Exit 3 means one of the events does not exist: if a retry exits 3, the first
attempt may already have deleted them. A deleted event's seq is never given to another
event.

An event is named by its seq in the current timeline (12), by a timeline and seq
(mongo-outage#12), or by its id or the start of it, in any timeline. Seqs and id starts
are for people; scripts pass full ids.

USAGE
  coroner event delete <event>... [flags]

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event delete 12 14 --dry-run
  $ coroner event delete mongo-outage-2026-09-11#12 --json

FLAGS
      --dry-run   Show what would be deleted, and delete nothing

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path        Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path            Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug              Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format      Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help               Show help for a command
      --json               Short for --format json
      --no-color           Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input           Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet              Print only essential output
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --tz zone            Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_edit.txt --
Change the fields of an event. --at and --ended are read as 'coroner event add' reads them,
and the times they resolve to are printed on stderr. Tags are removed before they are
added; adding a tag it has, or removing one it lacks, changes nothing. Pass none to
--source, --url, --author or --ended to clear it. Pass --text - to read the text from
stdin, which is never read otherwise. --attach adds a file to the event's attachments;
'coroner attachment delete' removes one.

An event is named by its seq in the current timeline (12), by a timeline and seq
(mongo-outage#12), or by its id or the start of it, in any timeline. Seqs and id starts
are for people; scripts pass full ids.

USAGE
  coroner event edit <event> [flags]

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event edit 12 --add-tag root-cause --remove-tag election --dry-run
  $ coroner event edit 12 --attach ./grafana-p99.png
  $ coroner event edit mongo-outage-2026-09-11#12 --at "2026-09-11T03:14:07.412Z" --source none

FLAGS
      --add-tag tag      Add a tag; repeat for more
      --approximate      Mark the time as a guess; --approximate=false unmarks it
      --at time          When it happened, as a time
      --attach file      Copy this file in as an attachment; repeat for more
      --author name      Who added it, as a name, or none
      --dry-run          Show the edited event, and change nothing
      --ended time       When it ended, as a time, or none
      --remove-tag tag   Remove a tag; repeat for more
      --source source    System, service or person it came from, as a source, or none
      --text text        New text, or - to read it from stdin
      --url url          Permalink to the log line or message, as a url, or none

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path        Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path            Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug              Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format      Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help               Show help for a command
      --json               Short for --format json
      --no-color           Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input           Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet              Print only essential output
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --tz zone            Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_link.txt --
Link the first event to the second. --kind says how they relate: led_to when the first led
to the second, same_as when both record one moment as two sources saw it, and copied_from
when the first was copied from the second. The events may be in different timelines; an
event cannot link to itself. From one event to another there is at most one link of each
kind, so another exits 4; a link in the opposite direction is a separate link. --dry-run
checks the link and adds nothing. 'coroner event view' shows an event's links, and exports
carry every link between two exported events.

An event is named by its seq in the current timeline (12), by a timeline and seq
(mongo-outage#12), or by its id or the start of it, in any timeline. Seqs and id starts
are for people; scripts pass full ids.

USAGE
  coroner event link <from-event> <to-event> --kind {led_to | same_as | copied_from} [flags]

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event link mongo-failover#12 checkout-latency#3 --kind led_to
  $ coroner event link 0199a1b2-7c1e 0199a1b2-7d40 --kind same_as --json

FLAGS
      --author name   Who is adding it, as a name (default: config author, then USER)
      --dry-run       Show the link as it would be added, and add nothing
      --kind kind     Link kind: {led_to | same_as | copied_from}

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path        Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path            Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug              Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format      Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help               Show help for a command
      --json               Short for --format json
      --no-color           Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input           Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet              Print only essential output
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --tz zone            Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_list.txt --
List the current timeline's events in the order they happened: by occurred at, then seq.

Repeat -t to overlay several timelines: their events merge into one list by occurred at,
then timeline id, then seq, and each text row starts with its timeline's name. --json lists
the selected timelines under timelines, and --format jsonl starts with a timeline line for
each, so an event's timeline_id names one of them. --overlay <name> reads the timelines an
overlay saved with 'coroner overlay create' holds, in place of -t.

--since is inclusive and --until exclusive. Repeated --source flags match any of them,
and repeated --tag flags must all match. Times with no offset are read, and times print,
in --tz, then the config file's default_timezone, then the local zone. Piped, the
fields are tab-separated.

USAGE
  coroner event list [flags]

EXAMPLES
  $ coroner event list --tag election --source mongod
  $ coroner event list --since "2026-09-11T03:00Z" --until "2026-09-11T04:00Z" --tag election --format jsonl
  $ coroner event list --contains stepdown --order desc --limit 20
  $ coroner event list -t mongo-failover -t checkout-latency --since 2026-09-10T14:00Z --json
  $ coroner event list --overlay checkout-incident --tag phase:detect

FLAGS
      --contains text   Keep events whose text contains this text, ignoring case
      --limit number    Show at most this number of events (default: every one)
      --order order     Order by occurred at, then timeline id, then seq, as an order: {asc | desc} (default "asc")
      --overlay name    Read the timelines of the overlay called name, instead of -t
      --since time      Keep events at or after this time
      --source source   Keep events from this source; repeat to match any of several
      --tag tag         Keep events with this tag; repeat to require all of them
      --until time      Keep events before this time

GLOBAL FLAGS
      --config path        Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path            Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug              Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format      Output format: {text | json | jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help               Show help for a command
      --json               Short for --format json
      --no-color           Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input           Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet              Print only essential output
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --tz zone            Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)

LEARN MORE
  Use `coroner <command> --help` for more information about a command.
  Report bugs and send feedback at https://github.com/macintacos/coroner/issues
-- coroner_event_unlink.txt --
Remove the link of --kind from the first event to the second, and print it as it was. A link
that does not exist exits 3. --dry-run shows the link it would remove instead.

An event is named by its seq in the current timeline (12), by a timeline and seq
(mongo-outage#12), or by its id or the start of it, in any timeline. Seqs and id starts
are for people; scripts pass full ids.

USAGE
